# Coding Conventions — NestJS

## Dependency Injection (DI)

Always inject via constructor — never instantiate services with `new`:

```typescript
// ✅ Correct
@Injectable()
export class OrderService {
  constructor(
    private readonly userService: UserService,
    @InjectRepository(Order)
    private readonly orderRepository: Repository<Order>,
  ) {}
}

// ❌ Wrong
const userService = new UserService(); // bypasses NestJS DI
```

---

## DTO Validation (class-validator)

All incoming data must go through a DTO with validation decorators:

```typescript
export class CreateUserDto {
  @IsEmail()
  email: string;

  @IsString()
  @MinLength(8)
  password: string;

  @IsOptional()
  @IsString()
  name?: string;
}
```

- Never validate manually in the controller — use `ValidationPipe` globally
- Every field that is optional must be decorated with `@IsOptional()`

---

## Exception Handling

Use NestJS built-in exceptions — never throw plain `Error`:

```typescript
// ✅ Correct
throw new NotFoundException(`User #${id} not found`);
throw new BadRequestException('Email already in use');
throw new UnauthorizedException();

// ❌ Wrong
throw new Error('User not found'); // no HTTP status code
```

---

## Response Pattern

Do not return raw entities — always map to a response DTO or plain object:

```typescript
// ✅ Correct
return { id: user.id, email: user.email, createdAt: user.createdAt };

// ❌ Wrong
return user; // may expose passwordHash, internalFields, etc.
```

---

## Naming Convention

| Type | Convention | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Module | PascalCase + `Module` | `UserModule`, `AuthModule` |
| Controller | PascalCase + `Controller` | `UserController` |
| Service | PascalCase + `Service` | `UserService` |
| DTO | PascalCase + `Dto` | `CreateUserDto`, `UpdateUserDto` |
| Entity | PascalCase | `User`, `Order` |
| File | kebab-case | `user.service.ts`, `create-user.dto.ts` |
